# Put a Luckblade in a character's hands and activate it three times, dumping
# the item menu before each activation and the message line after it. Read by
# tools/check_luckblade_plus.sh, finding PA-08-F10 of bd inc-tek.8.8.
#
# WHAT IS BEING MEASURED. The sword's magical plus, before and after each
# activation. The Luckblade charges one Plus for a wish and then prints
# "Wishes aren't implemented yet; sorry.", so a player paid an irreversible
# price for nothing. The ruling is that the game must refuse before it
# charges, which means the plus must be the SAME after three activations as
# it was before the first one.
#
# WHY THE ITEM MENU IS THE ORACLE. Item::xName appends the plus to the item's
# name whenever the item is known (src/Message.cpp:1300-1308), and it does so
# for an EF_NAMEONLY entity too -- the flag only chooses the base word, the
# plus is written after it either way. So the 'a' command's own menu, which
# the player reads before choosing what to activate, spells the number out:
# "Luckblade +3". No debug screen is needed, and no arithmetic. When the plus
# reaches zero the name loses its plus entirely, because xName writes nothing
# for GetPlus() == 0, so a drained sword reads plainly as "Luckblade".
#
# NOTHING IS IDENTIFIED HERE, and it does not need to be. Item::isActivatable
# returns false for anything not known to be magical (src/Item.cpp:939-941),
# so an unidentified Luckblade would never appear in the 'a' menu at all and
# the session would measure nothing. Wizard mode's Item Acquisition hands over
# an item that is already known -- the run's own inventory screen calls it
# "keen Luckblade +3 [+3, +3]" the moment it arrives -- unlike "Acquire
# Unidentified Item", which calls SetKnown(0) first (src/Debug.cpp:657-666).
# The check reads that name back and reports INCONCLUSIVE if the plus is
# missing, so a future change that stopped identifying it could not pass here
# by accident.
#
#   w y                    enter wizard mode and open its menu. Later 'w'
#                          presses need no 'y': the confirmation is asked once
#                          (src/Player.cpp:773-784).
#   Item Acquisition       RIGHT*10 walks the glyph row to specific weapons:
#   RIGHT*10               ItemGlyphs (src/Tables.cpp:320-330) puts -AI_WEAPON
#                          ten steps along.
#   @until "Holy Avenger"  the candidate list has no menu letters, so the
#     DOWN                 cursor is walked. Three rows show and the MIDDLE
#                          one is selected, so the run stops on the pass where
#                          the row BELOW the wanted one appears. Holy Avenger
#                          is the AI_WEAPON entity after Luckblade in
#                          lib/m_items.irh. The check does not trust that
#                          ordering: it reads the sword's name back off the
#                          item menu before believing any number.
#   ENTER 3 ENTER          take it, and answer "Enter Item Level:" with 3,
#                          which is the sword's own INITIAL_PLUS and the plus
#                          its page states (src/Debug.cpp:613-620).
#   b SPACE n s            the pickup drops the sword into the in-air slot and
#                          opens Inventory Mode. 'b' is the Weapon Hand -- the
#                          slot lettering skips 'd', which is the Drop key --
#                          SPACE swaps the sword into it (src/Managers.cpp:873).
#                          The Ready Hand is empty, so the manager then asks
#                          "Wield the keen Luckblade +3 two-handed?" and 'n'
#                          declines, which is what a short sword is for. 's'
#                          stows the starting knife that swap pushed into the
#                          air, because the manager will not close while
#                          anything is held there (src/Managers.cpp:835-848).
#   ESC                    leave the manager.
#   a                      the Activate command. Player::ItemMenu lists every
#                          activatable item (src/Player.cpp:1399-1401) and
#                          throws EV_ACTIVATE at the one chosen (:1495, :1531).
#   @dump:menu-N           the menu itself, with the sword's name and plus on
#                          it. This is the measurement.
#   a                      choose the menu's first entry, which activates the
#                          sword. It is a bare letter and not @choose because
#                          @choose matches from the start of the entry's text
#                          (src/Wposix.cpp:882-895) and the game writes the
#                          sword's damage state in front of its name: the row
#                          reads "[a] mildly damaged keen Luckblade +3".
#                          Matching that whole phrase would tie this script to
#                          a wear level. The letter is safe because the check
#                          reads the "[a]" row back off every menu dump and
#                          refuses to report anything unless that row names a
#                          Luckblade.
#   @dump:msg-N            the message line, where the refusal is printed.
#
# Four menus are dumped for three activations. menu-1 is the sword before any
# activation and menu-4 is the sword after all three, so the pair brackets the
# whole experiment; menu-2 and menu-3 show whether the loss is one Plus at a
# time. The final 'a' is answered with ESC, which leaves the menu without
# activating anything (src/TextTerm.cpp, MENU_ESC).

@include chargen.keys

w
y
@choose "Item Acquisition"
RIGHT*10
@until "Holy Avenger" DOWN
ENTER
3
ENTER
b
SPACE
n
s
@dump:wielded
ESC

a
@dump:menu-1
a
@dump:msg-1

a
@dump:menu-2
a
@dump:msg-2

a
@dump:menu-3
a
@dump:msg-3

a
@dump:menu-4
ESC
